Z||000002||SKIDMARK||12-31-2001||06:59 PM||||A couple of questions.<BR>How many miles on the car?<BR>Is it really smoke or just vapor?<BR>Is there oil all over the engine?||||reg||1
Z||000003||FiXXXerX||01-01-2002||02:21 AM||||The car has around 76000 miles on it, I don't remember off hand, but I would only be off by a couple of hundred. No, No oil on the engine, though I did notice that there is to much oil in the engine and plan on draining it on Wednsday and add new Oil and new Oil filter. It just looks like vapor.... it doesn't smell repugnent or anything, in fact I think its void of smell... but I just wanna know if its normal. ALSO, this car hasn't been run in a LONG time, so, I dunno how easy it should be to start or what I should do to bring it back up to speed, or what problem I may have resurecting it, and whats to be expected. The engine like I said, DID start, once and run under its own power, though it was idleing REALLY rough and when I pressed the accelerator it died. I did see that one spark plug was not actually screwed in, and, just went ahead and replaced them all, as I will be doing to the wires as well here in a few hours. What else can I do? And why won't she turn over? Like I said, there is way to much oil in the vehicle, like, the oil mark is around 2 and a half inches about normal operating range. I'm sorry if these questions seem rediculously simple, but, I'm just trying to learn some basic mechanical tips and things I SHOULD already know. But, I've only been working on cars since June of 2001, and the car I work on Primarily is a '84 Camaro Z28, Carburated and not fuel injected.... so, I'm struggleing here trying to pick up on how TBI works and how to get this engine running. I've never had to do anything like this before, and since I'm undertaking this all by myself (With the exception of your help), I can do nothing more than read the Haynes book and ask you guys. And since the Haynes book can't reply and give basic advice, I really only have one other route and, thats asking you guys and gals here on the forum. Welp, I'd appreciate any help you all can give me, and Happy New Year!!<P>Adam<P>1986 Fiero 2M4<P>------------------<BR>-= If the Front wheels don't leave the ground, its still broken =-||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||01-01-2002||06:57 AM||||Now you say that it won't 'turn over'?<BR>Or do you simply mean that it won't start?<BR>Just a short note on terms, when key is turned 'ON' with warning lights on the engine is not running or is at rest. With the key to 'start' the engine rotates or cranks or is turning over. If it does this correctly it just plain won't start or run. Don't worry about it as that is a common mistake made by many!<BR>If your engine turns over and will not start, but did run breifly though poorly, would tend to indicate at that time you had some spark.<BR>Since the car hasn't run is a long time, have you done any fuel system maintanince to it? You know, things like draining fuel tank adding fresh fuel, changing fuel filter and stuff like that there!<BR>The vapor you may have seen since the enging has not been run for a while may be blow by from the exhaust stroke from firing cylinders. This is very common when resurecting a basicly abandoned car. Begin to worry if the problem continues after it's been running a while. It may go away fast or it may take some time. Running a good fuel injector cleaner will dry out your tank and fuel system, clean up the injector some and assist cleaning out the ring grooves on the pistons to re-seat them.<BR>Make it run first!||||reg||1
